I wrote the public suite in `tests/public/test_patisserie.py`. `python3 sandbox/compile_check.py` reports no errors. All 11 tests fail with a runtime error, as expected while `src/main.py` raises.

**There are no sample tests.** `spec.md` has no sample inputs and outputs, and no constraints section. So every expected value here is derived, and all cases stay within what the statement itself requires: at least two cakes per pair chosen (2K ≤ N), K ≥ 1, and non-negative integer values. The 10^9 values come from the original AtCoder problem, not from this spec. The spec doesn't forbid them.

- **Answers worked out by hand (8 tests):**
  - the smallest input (N=2, K=1);
  - picking the best pair out of three;
  - a case where how the cakes are paired changes the answer (40 versus 20);
  - all values zero;
  - three cases with values of 10^9, whose totals of 2,000,000,000 to 4,000,000,000 exceed the 32-bit integer range;
  - several test cases in one input, to check that answers come out one per line in order.

  I also ran each of these through the brute force below, and it gave the same answers.
- **Answers from brute force (3 tests):** each test packs many small random cases (N ≤ 8) into one input. The brute force tries every way of choosing K separate pairs, so it is obviously correct at this size. One test uses small values, one uses values up to 10^9, and one uses K = N/2, so every cake must be paired. The random seeds are fixed, so the cases are the same on every run.

11 tests, in 1 file.
